course: politics
lesson: 9
title: Around the English-speaking world: Canada, Australia, Ireland, New Zealand
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Canada, Australia, Ireland and New Zealand all borrowed ideas from the British way of running a country, then made them their own, like a family recipe each cook changes a little.
explain: Canada's Parliament in Ottawa looks a bit like London's, with a House of Commons and an appointed Senate, but MPs speak English or French and interpreters turn one into the other as they talk.
explain: In Australia adults must vote or risk a small fine, they number the candidates 1, 2, 3 in order of who they like, and many polling places sell a "democracy sausage" in bread outside.
explain: Ireland has no king: its President lives in a big white house in a Dublin park and mostly does ceremonies, while the Taoiseach (said TEE-shuck) runs the government and answers to the Dáil (said DOYL); Irish counts can take days because votes move from one choice to the next.
explain: New Zealand voters get two votes, one for a local MP and one for a party, so Parliament looks like the way people voted, and there are Māori seats linked to the Treaty of Waitangi; elections come every three years there, four for a US president, and up to five in the UK.
fact: Canadians vote for MPs in the House of Commons in Ottawa, and the leader of the biggest party usually becomes prime minister; Canada's Senate is appointed, not elected.
fact: Canada has two official languages, English and French; MPs may speak either, interpreters translate as they speak, and laws are written in both.
fact: In Australia, voting in national elections is compulsory for adult citizens, so almost everyone takes part; in the USA, voting is a free choice.
fact: At many Australian polling places, schools and clubs sell sausages in bread to raise money; people call it the democracy sausage.
fact: In Australia, voters number the candidates in order of preference; if nobody has more than half, the last candidate is knocked out and their votes go to next choices, until someone has more than half.
fact: The Taoiseach is Ireland's head of government, like a prime minister, and the Dáil is the main elected house of Ireland's Parliament; the words come from Irish, one of Ireland's official languages.
fact: Ireland is a republic: its elected President is head of state, mostly for ceremonies, and lives at Áras an Uachtaráin in Phoenix Park, Dublin.
fact: Ireland uses the single transferable vote: voters rank candidates, each area elects several people, and votes are counted round by round, which can take days.
fact: Tallymen are party helpers who watch the Irish count and keep their own tally to guess the result early.
fact: New Zealand uses MMP (mixed-member proportional): each voter has an electorate vote for a local MP and a party vote, which decides each party's share of seats.
fact: New Zealand's Māori electorates, set up in 1867, are seats for voters on the Māori roll; the Treaty of Waitangi of 1840 is a founding agreement between Māori chiefs and the British Crown.
fact: New Zealand and Australia hold national elections at least every three years, the USA elects a president every four years, and the UK holds a general election at least every five years.
fact: People argue about these systems: some say ranking and MMP are fairer, others say they are slower, more complicated, or give small parties too much power.
exercise: Finn from Ireland sees Canada's Parliament in Ottawa and says, "It looks like London!" Why? | Canada's system grew from the British one, with a House of Commons | Canada's Parliament is just a copy built in Ottawa for tourists to photograph | The British Parliament moved to Ottawa | All parliaments in the world look exactly alike
exercise: How are members of Canada's Senate chosen? | They are appointed, not elected | They are elected every year by children | They win a national hockey tournament | They are the oldest people in each town
exercise: Canadian MPs switch between English and French. How do they understand each other? | Interpreters translate as they speak, and both are official languages | Every MP must speak only French on Mondays and only English on the other days | They don't, and nobody minds | They write notes to each other instead
exercise: Kai's dad in Brisbane says he'll be fined if he doesn't vote. Why? | Voting in national elections is compulsory in Australia | Brisbane charges a fine to everybody who stays at home on a Saturday | He must pay a fee at the polling place every time he votes | He forgot to buy a sausage at the polling place, which is the law
exercise: What is the democracy sausage? | A sausage in bread sold at Australian polling places to raise money | A sausage given only to the winning candidate at the end of the count | A special sausage that counts as a vote | A rude name for a politician
exercise: Grace's penpal sends a ballot with 1, 2, 3, 4 in the boxes. What does it mean? | Voters rank the candidates in order of who they like | Voters give each candidate a score for their looks | The voter voted for four different winners | The numbers show how many times they voted
exercise: In Australian preferential voting, what happens if nobody has more than half? | The last candidate is knocked out and their votes go to next choices | The candidate with the most posters up around the town is the winner | The election is cancelled and held next year | The oldest candidate is declared the winner
exercise: Oisin's English cousin asks about "the tea-what in the what". What are the Taoiseach and the Dáil? | Ireland's head of government and its main elected house | A type of Irish tea and a kind of cake | Ireland's king and his castle | Ireland's top judge and supreme court
exercise: Chloe hears Ireland's President lives in the big white house in a park. "But I thought Ireland had a Taoiseach!" What is true? | Ireland has both: a President for ceremonies and a Taoiseach to run the government | The President and the Taoiseach are the same person, who simply uses two names for two jobs | The Taoiseach lives there and the President has no home | Ireland has a king who lives there instead
exercise: Why does Ireland have no king or queen? | Ireland is a republic | The king is on holiday | Ireland has two kings who share | Ireland's king lives in Canada
exercise: Why does Oisin's Irish election count go on for days? | Votes are ranked and counted round by round, moving to next choices | The counters only work one hour a day | Every single vote must be read out on the radio, one by one, before it counts | The tallymen keep losing the ballot papers
exercise: What do tallymen do at an Irish count? | Watch the count for their party and keep their own tally | Count the votes and decide the winner alone | Hand out sausages to the voters | Carry the ballot boxes on their heads
exercise: Aroha's mum gives her party vote to one party and her electorate vote to a different person. Is that allowed? | Yes, New Zealand's MMP gives each voter two separate votes | No, she must vote for the same party twice, or both votes are thrown out | No, only MPs are allowed two votes | Yes, but only one of the votes is counted
exercise: What does the party vote decide in New Zealand? | How many seats each party gets overall | Which party gets the nicest building | Who is the King of New Zealand | Which day the election is held
exercise: Why does New Zealand have Māori seats in Parliament? | To make sure Māori, the first people of New Zealand, have a voice | Because Māori voters are not allowed to vote in any of the other seats in the country | Because the seats are for visitors from abroad | Because the King asked for them in 2020
exercise: What is the Treaty of Waitangi? | An 1840 founding agreement between Māori chiefs and the British Crown | A rule that says New Zealand must vote every year | A sports deal between New Zealand and Australia, signed after a rugby match in 1840 | A treaty that ended the American Revolution
exercise: How often do New Zealand, the USA and the UK hold their main elections? | At least every three years, every four for a US president, and at least every five | Every single year in all three countries, on the same day, so they can compare results | Every ten years in all three countries | Only when the King asks for one
test: An Aussie cousin texts, "Election day, chur! Snagged a democracy sanga at the school." ("Snagged" means got; a "sanga" is a sandwich.) What did he get? | A sausage in bread sold at the polling place | A free vote for buying a sandwich | A prize for voting first | A ticket to meet the prime minister
test: Your American friend says, "Australia fines people for not voting? That's so sus, it's a free country." Which is the balanced view? | Some say compulsory voting makes the result show everyone; others say people should be free not to vote | Australia is not a democracy because of it, and everyone there agrees the fines should stop at once | Every country in the world fines non-voters | The fine is so big that people go to prison
test: A Kiwi kid says, "Sweet as, Mum ticked Greens for party and the Labour bloke for our local." ("Sweet as" means fine.) What does this show? | New Zealand voters can split their two votes between different choices | Her mum has voted illegally twice, and both of her votes will now be thrown away | Only the party vote will be counted, so the tick for the Labour bloke is wasted | The local vote decides the whole government and the party vote is just for fun
test: A Dubliner says, "Ah, the count's a grand bit of craic, the tallymen have it called already." What does he mean? | Party helpers watching the count are guessing the result early | The tallymen are the official counters | The election was decided before voting | The President has called the result himself
test: What would happen if Australia stopped compulsory voting? | Fewer people might vote, so the result might show fewer people's views | The election would no longer have any candidates | Everyone would be forced to vote twice instead, once in the morning and once at night | The democracy sausage would become illegal
test: Your cousin from England says, "TEE-shuck? Sounds like a sneeze, mate." Which is the polite and correct answer? | It is the Irish word for Ireland's head of government | It is a type of Irish sweet | It is the Irish name for the King | It is a rude word in Irish
test: In a game, Canada's Parliament has only English signs and French is banned. What should be fixed? | Canada's Parliament works in both English and French | Canada's only language is French | Canada's Parliament is in London | Canada does not have a parliament
test: A boy in Wellington says, "Why do Māori get special seats? That's unfair, innit." Which answer gives both sides? | Some say they protect a voice for the first people; others say everyone should vote on one roll | The seats are only for tourists from Australia | Māori are not allowed to vote anywhere else at all, so without these special seats they would have no vote | Nobody has ever argued about the Māori seats
test: Your American cousin says, rather formally, "In the event that no candidate secures a majority of first preferences, the lowest candidate shall be excluded." Where does that rule come from? | Australian preferential voting | The US Electoral College in Washington | UK first-past-the-post voting | The Irish President's election only
test: A clever puzzle: in an Australian seat, Ana has 40 first votes, Ben 35, Cal 25. Cal is knocked out and 20 of Cal's voters put Ben next, 5 put Ana. Who wins? | Ben, with 55 | Ana, with 45 votes | Cal, with 25 | Nobody; it is a tie
test: An Irish teacher says, in the old style, "The Oireachtas shall convene." What is the Oireachtas? | The full Irish Parliament | Ireland's President's house in the park | An Irish election count | A tallyman's own notebook
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the system of voting employed in elections to the House of Representatives of Australia? | Electors rank candidates in order of preference, and votes are transferred until one candidate secures a majority | Electors mark a single cross, and the candidate with the most crosses is elected, irrespective of whether a majority is secured | Electors cast one vote for a party and one for a local member | Electors are not required to attend, and preferences are not recorded
exam: Identify the person who exercises the functions of head of government in Ireland. | The Taoiseach | The President of Ireland | The Ceann Comhairle | The monarch of the United Kingdom
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the mixed-member proportional system of New Zealand. | Each elector possesses an electorate vote and a party vote, the latter determining the proportion of seats | Each elector possesses a single vote cast exclusively for a party leader | Seats are allocated by the British Crown according to population, the electors possessing no vote whatsoever | Seats are allocated exclusively to members of Māori electorates
exam: It is given that Canada has two official languages. Ascertain the consequence for the proceedings of its Parliament. | Members may speak in English or French, with interpretation provided and laws enacted in both | Members are obliged to speak exclusively in English, French being permitted only in the provinces | Members are obliged to speak exclusively in French | Proceedings are conducted in Latin to avoid preference
